# Hallmark Design Skill - Frontend Development Rules

## Overview
Use the Hallmark skill for all frontend development to create beautiful, non-templated UIs that look made, not generated. The skill is located at `.agents/skills/hallmark/SKILL.md`.

## When to Use Hallmark

### Always Use For:
- Building new landing pages, marketing pages, or app interfaces
- Creating dashboard layouts and data visualization pages
- Designing component libraries and design systems
- Redesigning existing pages to improve visual quality
- Auditing designs for AI-generated "slop" patterns

### Use the Appropriate Verb:

#### Default (Design Flow)
For new page creation - just describe what you want to build.
```
Example: "Build a SaaS landing page for an AI analytics tool"
```

#### `hallmark audit <target>`
Score existing designs against anti-pattern lists without making changes.
```
Example: "hallmark audit src/pages/landing.tsx"
```

#### `hallmark redesign <target> [--mood <name>]`
Redesign the visual structure while preserving routes, content, and brand.
```
Example: "hallmark redesign src/pages/pricing.tsx --mood editorial"
```

#### `hallmark study <screenshot | URL>`
Extract design DNA from reference images or live websites.
```
Example: "hallmark study https://example.com"
Example: "hallmark study screenshot.png"
```

## Core Principles

### 1. Structural Variety
- Never reuse the same macrostructure (Bento Grid, Marquee Hero, etc.) across pages
- Rotate through 21 different page structures to avoid template-like repetition
- Each page should feel like a different site, not a color-swap

### 2. Typography First
- Most hero sections should be typography-only (no unnecessary imagery)
- Use distinctive display + body font pairings (not single-font pages)
- Never italicize headings - it's an AI tell

### 3. Honest Copy
- Never invent metrics, testimonials, or fake data
- Use placeholders or ask for real content
- No "+47% conversion" or "trusted by 50,000+ teams" unless provided

### 4. Design Tokens
- All colors use OKLCH format with CSS custom properties
- All fonts reference named tokens (`var(--font-display)`)
- Never inline raw hex/rgb values or font names mid-render

### 5. Mobile Responsiveness
- Verify at 320px, 375px, 414px, and 768px widths
- No horizontal scroll (use `overflow-x: clip` on html/body)
- All interactive text must fit on one line

### 6. Interactive States
- Design all 8 states: default, hover, focus, active, disabled, loading, error, success
- Focus rings must be instantly visible (never animated)
- Use optimistic updates over loading spinners

## Pre-flight Protocol

Before building, Hallmark will:
1. Scan existing fonts, colors, spacing, and frameworks
2. Preserve your design system if one exists
3. Ask for audience, use case, and tone (or infer if you say "go ahead")

## What Hallmark Produces

### For Pages:
- Complete HTML + CSS with proper semantic structure
- `tokens.css` with all design system tokens
- `.hallmark/log.json` tracking macrostructure history
- Macrostructure stamp in CSS comments

### For Components:
- Single component file matching project conventions
- 8-state demo wrapper for testing
- Token-based styling (no inline colors)

## Design System Management

### Lock a Design System
After building a page you're happy with:
```
"lock the system"
```
This creates `design.md` - a portable design system that subsequent pages will follow.

### Multi-Page Projects
If `design.md` exists, all pages share the same theme (inverted diversification).
Without `design.md`, each page gets a different structure and theme.

## Common Workflows

### New SaaS Landing Page
```
"Build a landing page for [product] that [does X]. Audience: [who]. Use case: [action]. Tone: modern-minimal"
```

### Component Creation
```
"Create a button component with all 8 states following our design system"
```

### Redesign Existing Page
```
"hallmark redesign src/pages/about.tsx --mood editorial"
```

### Study Reference Design
```
"hallmark study https://stripe.com/climate"
```
Then: "Build with this DNA" or "Lock the DNA" to create design.md

### Audit for Quality
```
"hallmark audit src/pages/homepage.tsx"
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

Hallmark automatically prevents these AI tells:
- Fake browser chrome, phone frames, or IDE windows
- Invented metrics and testimonials
- Mid-render token improvisation
- Italic display headers
- Section tags like "01 · THE TOUR"
- Two-column hanging headers
- Default Specimen macrostructure overuse
- Generic N1a nav and Ft3 footer on every page

## Quality Gates

Every output passes 58 slop-test gates covering:
- Visual hierarchy and spacing
- Typography purity
- Color contrast (WCAG AAA preferred)
- Interactive state coverage
- Mobile responsiveness
- Microinteraction discipline
- Structural variety

## Files Created by Hallmark

- `.hallmark/log.json` - Diversification history
- `.hallmark/preflight.json` - Cached project scan
- `tokens.css` - Design system tokens
- `design.md` - Locked design system (opt-in)

## Integration with Existing Projects

Hallmark respects:
- Existing font stacks (next/font, Google Fonts, etc.)
- Current color palettes (OKLCH, HSL, hex in :root)
- Motion libraries (framer-motion, gsap, etc.)
- Spacing scales (Tailwind, CSS custom properties)
- Framework conventions (Next.js, Astro, Vue, Svelte)

Never overwrites global stylesheets unless explicitly requested.

## Tips for Best Results

1. **Be specific about tone** - "Clean and modern" isn't a tone. Use: editorial, brutalist, soft, utilitarian, luxury, playful, technical, or austere.

2. **Provide real content** - The better your copy, the better the design. Hallmark won't invent fake data.

3. **Let it diversify** - Don't request the same macrostructure repeatedly. Trust the rotation.

4. **Start simple** - Typography-only heroes are often stronger than image-heavy ones.

5. **Review tokens** - Check `tokens.css` to see your design system values clearly.

